.\" Hand-written. The event list and the ABI number must match
.\" mc_common::plugin — a tier-1 test asserts they do.
.TH MC-PLUGINS 5 "" "mc" "mc manual"
.SH NAME
mc-plugins \- plugin manifest format for mc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /usr/lib/mc/plugins.d/*.toml
.SH DESCRIPTION
A plugin is a
.B .deb
that drops a TOML manifest into
.I /usr/lib/mc/plugins.d/
and an executable into
.IR /usr/libexec/mc/ .
.BR mc (1)
reads every manifest in that directory at startup, in filename order, and
invokes the executable across a process boundary.
.PP
Out of process, rather than a shared library, because Rust has no stable ABI: a
.BR dlopen -based
plugin would have to be rebuilt in lockstep with core, which defeats the point
of "adding a capability is installing another package". One fork and exec per
invocation is nothing at command-line latency.
.PP
A manifest that fails to load is reported, never silently skipped \(em
.B mc plugins
prints the problem. A plugin whose command was expected and has vanished is
exactly the case a silent skip would hide.
.SH FIELDS
.TP
.B abi
.RI ( "integer, required" )
The plugin interface version this plugin was built against. Core implements
.BR 1 .
A manifest declaring anything else is refused by name, and its commands and
hooks do not run.
.IP
This replaces a versioned
.BR Depends: .
The arrangement it replaced had a plugin's postinst source another package's
private shell library, so a missed version-floor bump left dpkg configuring the
plugin against a library without the function it called. Core reads the number
instead.
.TP
.B name
.RI ( "string, required" )
The plugin's name. Also the naming rule for its manual page:
.B mc man <command>
looks for
.BI mc- name (1),
so a plugin named
.B backup
must ship
.BR mc-backup (1).
.TP
.B bin
.RI ( "path, required" )
The executable. Conventionally
.IR /usr/libexec/mc/mc-<name> ,
which is deliberately not on
.BR PATH :
it is invoked by core and is not a command surface anyone should script
against.
.SH "[[commands]]"
One table per subcommand contributed. Only a registered name is dispatchable \(em
resolving to some executable is not sufficient, or an internal entry point
would become callable from the command line, skipping the guards, the lock and
the configuration loading its real entry point performs.
.TP
.B name
The subcommand as typed:
.B name = "backup"
makes
.B mc backup
work.
.TP
.B about
One-line description, shown by
.BR "mc plugins" .
.PP
Core invokes it as:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.RI < bin "> command <" name "> [args...]"
.fi
.RE
.PP
The plugin enforces its own privilege guard. Core does not refuse on its behalf:
only the plugin knows whether the subcommand reads or writes.
.SH "[[hooks]]"
One table per lifecycle event.
.TP
.B event
One of:
.RS
.TP
.B pre-start
Before the unit is started.
.TP
.B pre-stop
Before the server is told to stop.
.TP
.B pre-backup
Before a backup archives the world.
.TP
.B post-backup
After a backup, whether or not it succeeded.
.TP
.B post-install
After a server is installed.
.TP
.B post-upgrade
After a server is upgraded.
.RE
.IP
An unrecognised event is a manifest error, not a silent no-op: a hook declared
as
.B pre-stopp
would otherwise install cleanly and never run.
.TP
.B fatal
.RI ( "boolean, default " false )
Whether a non-zero exit aborts the operation around it. Contributing a step
must not become a way to break the core path, so this is off unless asked for.
.IP
.B pre-stop
and
.B post-backup
may never be fatal, and a manifest that says otherwise is refused at load time.
A shutdown does not stop because a warning could not be delivered, and a
backup's save-on must run even after the archive failed \(em a live server left
with saving disabled loses everything since the last flush.
.PP
Core invokes a hook as:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.RI < bin "> hook <" event ">"
.fi
.RE
.PP
with a JSON payload on standard input. Every hook registered for an event runs,
in filename order, even when an earlier one failed: they are independent
contributions, not a pipeline.
.SH "[[providers]]"
One table per artifact kind the plugin can install from.
.TP
.B kind
.RB ( source )
The only kind today: something that can populate a staging directory from a
file the operator names.
.TP
.B name
The provider's name, shown by
.BR "mc plugins" .
.TP
.B extensions
File extensions claimed, without the dot.
.B mc install pack.mrpack
routes to whichever provider claims
.BR mrpack .
.PP
Core invokes it as:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.RI < bin "> provide <" pack "> <" staging-dir ">"
.fi
.RE
.PP
The provider populates the staging directory and prints one JSON object on
standard output \(em
.BR server_type ,
.B minecraft_version
and
.B java_version
\(em with everything human-facing on standard error, so core can parse the
answer without stripping progress lines.
.SH "WHAT MUST NOT MOVE INTO A PLUGIN"
The lock, the EULA gate, ownership of
.IR /opt/minecraft ,
and the system-managed keys of
.IR server.properties .
A plugin contributes a
.IR step ;
core keeps the ordering that makes the step safe. That is why a modpack
provider only fetches and stages: core re-applies the RCON keys afterwards, so
a pack cannot enable RCON with a password of its choosing.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
.RS
abi  = 1
name = "hello"
bin  = "/usr/libexec/mc/mc\-hello"

[[commands]]
name  = "hello"
about = "Say hello"

[[hooks]]
event = "post\-install"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Installed as a
.B .deb
that depends on
.BR mc-server ,
alongside
.I /usr/share/man/man1/mc-hello.1
so that
.B mc man hello
has something to open.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/lib/mc/plugins.d/
Manifests. Read in filename order, which is what makes hook order the same on
every machine.
.TP
.I /usr/libexec/mc/
Plugin executables.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mc (1),
.BR mc-config (5),
.BR mc-rcon (1),
.BR mc-backup (1),
.BR mc-mrpack (1)
